adjudication.rationale:
In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the only authorization check is:
```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET)
  return res.status(403)
```
This uses normal `!==` string comparison for a shared secret. On an endpoint that can create/update credentials for any `userId`/`appSlug` in the request body, this is a verifiable security weakness: non-constant-time comparisons can leak timing information about the secret and are typically replaced with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` (after normalizing to buffers of equal length) to avoid timing attacks.

In the newly added API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the webhook secret check is implemented as a direct inequality comparison:

```ts
if (
  req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !==
  process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET
) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This is not a constant-time comparison (it relies on JS string/primitive comparison semantics), so it does not provide timing-attack resistance. The finding correctly identifies that the diff introduces a non-constant-time secret comparison instead of using something like `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on normalized byte buffers.

In the new API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the webhook secret is validated via a direct inequality check:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This is a non-constant-time comparison of a shared secret. For secrets used as authenticators, using `crypto.timingSafeEqual` (with appropriate type/length checks) is the standard mitigation against timing attacks.

Also, once this check is bypassed, the endpoint allows the caller to choose `userId` and `appSlug` and then update/create that user’s credential in the database, so the impact of a successful secret-guess (or leak) is indeed arbitrary credential overwrite for any user ID.

So the finding points to a real security weakness present in the diff (non-constant-time secret comparison on a high-impact endpoint).

The new helper `parseRefreshTokenResponse` explicitly does:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

This is then used in `packages/app-store/office365calendar/lib/CalendarService.ts`:

```ts
const tokenResponse = parseRefreshTokenResponse(responseJson, refreshTokenResponseSchema);
o365AuthCredentials = { ...o365AuthCredentials, ...(tokenResponse.success && tokenResponse.data) };
await prisma.credential.update(...)
```

When the provider’s refresh response omits `refresh_token` (common), `parseRefreshTokenResponse` injects the literal string, and the spread merge overwrites the previously valid `o365AuthCredentials.refresh_token` with `"refresh_token"` before persisting. That will break subsequent refresh attempts until re-authentication. (GoogleCalendar has additional issues, but the finding as stated is directly verifiable from the diff.)
